{
  "raw": {
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=122050": {
      "title": "Political Science - Adjunct Pool",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Political Science - Adjunct Pool Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Political Science - Adjunct Pool Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Political Science - Adjunct Pool Category: Day and Evening Adjunct Pools Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Mar 31, 2020 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: PT No Benefits Position ID: 122050 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a vibrant public university located in the residential west side of Worcester, Massachusetts, the second largest city in New England and home to 38,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Situated on a compact 58-acre campus, the University offers 50 undergraduate and graduate academic programs to more than 6,400 students annually. The Princeton Review ranks us as one of the 75 \"Best Value\" public colleges in the country, as well as a \"Best in the Northeast\" college. Of the 13 undergraduate public universities in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, Worcester State University is one of only three to earn the latter distinction, which is largely based on academic quality and student surveys. Our tag line, \"Change the way the world works,\" speaks to our commitment to graduating students who are not only prepared for work or further study, but who also have learned how they can make a difference. With a marketing slogan of \"Academic excellence at an affordable price,\" the University strives to provide the best academic experience to students from all walks of life. Job Description: The History and Political Science Department seeks qualified individuals to join our Political Science adjunct hiring pool to express their interest in future, part-time teaching assignments at Worcester State.  Ph.D preferred; ABD considered. Typical course assignments are 100-level introductory courses such as: United States government, global politics, legal studies, and/or political theory.  There is a possibility of teaching electives as listed in the current catalog as well. Requirements: PhD in Political Science or related field preferred; ABD considered; MA degree required. College-level teaching experience is preferred. Experience with distance learning preferred. Must have a laptop and reliable internet access. Additional Information: Worcester State University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Women and minorities are strongly encouraged to apply. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Application submission to the part time adjunct faculty pool is open to qualified professionals who possess skills, education, and experience in academic and/or professional areas. Adjunct faculty postings are for potential part-time, non-benefited, teaching positions during the 2023-2024 academic year. The applicant pool will be reviewed by the department chair, when searching for candidates to fill the position. Applicants for adjunct positions will not be notified of their status unless they are contacted by the department chair or their designee. Applicants should withdraw their application from the pool if they are no longer interested. Application Instructions: Please submit a CV, cover letter (noting interest in day, evening, or both), and one letter of reference dated within the last 6 months. Educational background will be verified through the National Student Clearinghouse or through copies of original, sealed transcripts if selected for hire. Documents which cannot be uploaded to one's account may be sent via fax to 508.929.8169 or by mail to the following address: Director of Human Resources Worcester State University 486 Chandler Street Worcester MA 01602-2597 Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205772": {
      "title": "Assistant Director, Payment Services Operations Manager",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Assistant Director, Payment Services Operations Manager Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Director, Payment Services Operations Manager Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Director, Payment Services Operations Manager Category: Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 348-Purchasing/Accounts Payable Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Oct 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Salary Type: Annually Position ID: 205772 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a liberal arts and sciences university with a long tradition of academic excellence dating back to 1874, as well as an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer that seeks to reflect the diversity of its community. Our workforce is dedicated to academic quality, student-centered programming, engaged citizenship, open exchanges of ideas, diversity and inclusiveness, and civility and integrity. We are looking for job applicants who share these values and commitment to our students. Worcester State is centrally located in Massachusetts, about an hour's drive from Boston, Springfield, and Providence, Rhode Island. Our 58-acre campus is nestled in the residential northwest side of Worcester--the second largest city in New England and home to 37,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Over 6,000 of those students attend Worcester State--approximately 5,300 are undergraduates and 1,000 are graduate students. Learn more about us at worcester.edu/about . Job Description: OFFICIAL TITLE: Assistant Director, Payment Services Operations Manager SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Director, Procure to Pay Services SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Accounts Payable Staff General Statement of Duties: The Assistant Director, Payment Services Operations Manager is responsible for day-to-day operations of all payments to vendors and suppliers for the university, and oversight of the accounts payable staff. The incumbent will manage the accounts payable function and the encumbrance-to-payment process in the Massachusetts Financial Management System, Mosaic (formerly MMARS). They will assist the Director of Procure to Pay Services in planning, directing, and managing the university's accounts payable processes. Responsibilities: (E) = Essential (E) Manages the accounts payable and encumbrance process for critical repairs and small repairs that need to be recorded in the state accounting system (Mosaic). Includes reconciling the local accounts/funds and managing accounts payable or similar accounting systems for all bills related to University's services. (E) Collaborates with the Accounting Office and Controller on a regular basis to ensure processes are running efficiently as well as identifying needs for improvement. (E) Collaborates with the CFO and Director of P2P on utility payments and net metering credits. (E) Responsible for all 1099 processing on an annual basis, W9, and ACH vendor verification. (E) Assists in resolving all issues that concern the department during the fiscal year, including the opening and closing of the state accounting system, Mosaic, and other University payment systems. (E) Assists the Director of Procure to Pay Services by working with the Finance and Accounting Units to ensure that funds are properly committed/encumbered during the fiscal year and that funds are re-allocated/obligated as needed. (E) Ensures that vendor/supplier invoices reflect the costs/prices offered on awarded contracts. (E) Manages the Accounts Payable staff, including ensuring that annual performance evaluations are completed in a timely and comprehensive manner, identifying the training needs of staff, and providing any necessary training. (E) Supports the vouching of vendor/supplier invoices as needed by the Department. (E) Verifies the accuracy of vendor invoices and confirms the correct GL account and unit and source codes. (E) Responds to external vendors and internal managers with AP inquiries. (E) Monitors the timely completion of payments, the application of payments, and processing, verifying, and reconciling invoices. (E) Reviews weekly vendor payment reports for accuracy and routine scanning of documentation for audit compliance. (E) Communicates with vendors on billing issues as well as providing technical assistance to vendors and employees to ensure superior customer service and compliance with University rules and regulations. (E) Participates in the development and implementation of the University's accounts payable policies and procedures. (E) Proactively identifies solutions for process improvements and implements effective solutions. (E) Supports ongoing department initiatives which include moving to paperless, cross-training, standardized/routine reporting, and performance management. (E) Prepares and disseminates information internally regarding payment statuses and processes; maintains related electronic and hard-copy files. (E) Demonstrates civility and professional, customer-service oriented behavior, worthy of emulation by other staff and students. (E) Responsible for contributing to the WSU Strategic Plan. (E) Responsible for contributing to Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action objectives. Performs other duties as assigned. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Business Administration, Accounting, Finance, or similar field or an equivalent combination of relevant education, training, and experience. At least five years of experience in accounting and/or accounts payable and two years of experience managing/supervising employees (or equivalent combination of education and work experience). Proven ability to manage and supervise, including planning and assigning work according to the nature of the job to be accomplished, the capabilities of subordinates, and available resources, controlling work through periodic reviews and/or evaluations. Ability to create, analyze, and interpret financial information and scenarios. Ability to identify and solve problems. Ability to work collaboratively with internal and external constituents, including cross-divisionally and cross-departmentally. Ability to successfully pass a thorough background check and CORI check. Ability to perform the essential functions of the position with or without reasonable accommodation. Preferred Qualifications: Experience in applying generally accepted accounting principles (GAAP), financial accounting theory and practice, and experience preferred with governmental accounting standards (GASB). CPA certification. Experience within a complex and large public higher education or non-profit organization. Ability to independently and accurately query and extract data from relational databases for multiple business purposes and convert to functional report formats and explain conclusions. Knowledge and understanding of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts reporting system (MMARS/Mosaic) and state appropriations. Advanced knowledge of  Excel, Gmail, Accounting and ERP software. Experience with Projects Accounting or another similar module. Experience with implementation of new billing/platform systems. Knowledge and understanding of state finance law as well as payment and procurement regulations. Additional Information: This is a full-time, benefited, exempt, APA professional position. The firm annual salary range for this position is $87,000.00- $92,000.00, commensurate with relevant professional experience and education. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Worcester State University offers a competitive benefits package that includes: a choice of 8 different pre-taxed, health insurance plan options a choice of pension or 401(a), and supplemental plan retirement options life insurance up to 8x the annual salary long-term disability coverage dental insurance 20 vacation days accrued per year 15 sick days accrued per year 5 personal days per year paid holidays tuition remission for self, spouse, and dependent children and more... We are an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Members of traditionally underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Application Instructions: Applicants must apply online and may do so by visiting www.worcester.edu/careers, and submitting a cover letter, resume, and the contact information of three professional references. For assistance with uploading materials, log a ticket via the HelpDesk link, or contact us directly at 508-929-8053. URL: www.worcester.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205769": {
      "title": "Staff Assistant, Facilities Operations",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Staff Assistant, Facilities Operations Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Staff Assistant, Facilities Operations Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Staff Assistant, Facilities Operations Category: Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 350-Facilities Operation Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Oct 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Salary Type: Annually Position ID: 205769 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a liberal arts and sciences university with a long tradition of academic excellence dating back to 1874, as well as an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer that seeks to reflect the diversity of its community. Our workforce is dedicated to academic quality, student-centered programming, engaged citizenship, open exchanges of ideas, diversity and inclusiveness, and civility and integrity. We are looking for job applicants who share these values and commitment to our students. Worcester State is centrally located in Massachusetts, about an hour's drive from Boston, Springfield, and Providence, Rhode Island. Our 58-acre campus is nestled in the residential northwest side of Worcester--the second largest city in New England and home to 37,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Over 6,000 of those students attend Worcester State--approximately 5,300 are undergraduates and 1,000 are graduate students. Learn more about us at worcester.edu/about . Job Description: OFFICIAL TITLE: Staff Assistant, Facilities Operations SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Executive Director for Facilities Operations and Capital Planning SUPERVISION EXERCISED: May supervise student employees and volunteers General Statement of Duties: The Staff Assistant provides comprehensive administrative and fiscal support to the Facilities Department. This position manages day-to-day budget, purchasing, and project accounting activities, including capital and deferred maintenance projects. This position serves as a primary customer service representative for the department, responding to inquiries, coordinating with vendors, and collaborating with departments across the University. The role requires strong organizational, accounting, financial, and communication skills, as well as the ability to manage multiple priorities independently. Responsibilities: (E) = Essential 1. Financial, Budget, and Purchasing Operations (E) Create and manage blanket orders and purchase orders for all Facilities budget centers, including State-supported capital and deferred maintenance projects. (E) Process all department invoices, ensuring correct budget centers, object codes, and required documentation for accurate and timely payment. (E) Track purchase orders, blanket orders, expenses, and draw-down values for capital and deferred maintenance projects. (E) Process payment applications for University bid projects. (E) Prepare monthly budget-to-actual reports for budget managers; identify funding deficiencies and recommend and process budget transfers. (E) Assist budget managers with fiscal year-end close in accordance with the University fiscal calendar. (E) Prepare ad hoc financial and budget reports for department leadership and staff. (E) Provide financial metrics related to campus facilities utility, maintenance, and usage costs. 2. Project Accounting and Records Management (E) Create, maintain, and reconcile all capital and deferred maintenance project records.  Ensure those projects in the Project Accounting system are current and accurate. (E) Reconcile project accounts annually with the Senior Financial Accountant - Projects Accounting in Fiscal Affairs and provide all required supporting documentation. (E) Maintain historical records of purchase orders and invoices. (E) Maintain data base of warranties, O&M manuals, specifications, plans, and related electronic files. (E) Maintain organized storage of project records in the Facilities plan room; coordinate back-scanning and plan conversion to CADD as needed. 3. Vendor, Contract, and Interdepartmental Coordination (E) Coordinate with vendors to obtain required documentation and ensure compliance with University and State procurement laws. (E) Maintain Facilities vendor contract databases, including contract terms, values, durations.  Review invoicing to ensure compliance service frequency and costs.  Resolve discrepancies with vendors. (E) Maintain working relationships with Accounts Payable/Accounts Receivable to facilitate timely payments and resolve accounting discrepancies. (E) Maintain relationships with local, state, and federal agencies to exchange information and coordinate activities or site visits. 4. Reporting and Department Support (E) Provide monthly reports to trades and grounds staff on blanket orders, purchase orders, and available funding. (E) Create and maintain spreadsheets to track costs and financial data as requested by University leadership. (E) Create and prepare all departmental travel authorization requests and expense reimbursements. (E) Schedule meetings and maintain department emergency contact lists and organizational charts. (E) Coordinate department uniform purchases, including sizing, vendor communication, and distribution in accordance with collective bargaining agreements. (E) Serve as backup to the Clerk IV by triaging service calls and creating work orders. (E) Report to campus during facilities emergencies to provide administrative support as needed. 5. Professional Conduct and Institutional Responsibilities (E) Demonstrate professionalism, civility, and a customer-service-oriented approach. (E) Contribute to the University Strategic Plan and Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action objectives. Perform other duties as assigned. Requirements: Required Qualifications: At least three (3) years of full-time (or equivalent part-time) experience in office management, office administration, business administration, or business management, including responsibilities such as purchasing, budgeting, accounting, records management, contract administration; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Proficiency with spreadsheet, word processing, and presentation software (e.g., Microsoft Excel, Word, PowerPoint, Google Workspace). Demonstrated knowledge of accounting principles, including University and State accounting practices. Knowledge of State procurement policies. Excellent oral and written communication and customer service skills. Experience working with diverse populations and the ability to maintain effective working relationships. Strong organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple priorities independently. Ability to perform essential functions with or without reasonable accommodation. Ability to successfully pass a background and CORI check. Preferred Qualifications: Associate's degree in Accounting or Business Administration. Experience supporting or managing facilities or operational budgets. Experience with Project Accounting systems and work order management software (e.g., School Dude). Knowledge of institutional facilities trades and materials (e.g., HVAC, electrical, plumbing, carpentry). Experience working in a collective bargaining environment. Additional Information: This is a full-time, benefited, exempt, APA professional position. The firm annual salary range for this position is $72,000.00- $75,000.00, commensurate with relevant professional experience and education. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Worcester State University offers a competitive benefits package that includes: a choice of 8 different pre-taxed, health insurance plan options a choice of pension or 401(a), and supplemental plan retirement options life insurance up to 8x the annual salary long-term disability coverage dental insurance 20 vacation days accrued per year 15 sick days accrued per year 5 personal days per year paid holidays tuition remission for self, spouse, and dependent children and more... We are an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Members of traditionally underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Application Instructions: Applicants must apply online and may do so by visiting www.worcester.edu/careers, and submitting a cover letter, resume, and the contact information of three professional references. For assistance with uploading materials, log a ticket via the HelpDesk link, or contact us directly at 508-929-8053. URL: www.worcester.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205761": {
      "title": "Staff Assistant, Design Artist",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Staff Assistant, Design Artist Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Staff Assistant, Design Artist Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Staff Assistant, Design Artist Category: Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 824-Publications/Printing Services Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Oct 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Salary Type: Annually Position ID: 205761 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a liberal arts and sciences university with a long tradition of academic excellence dating back to 1874, as well as an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer that seeks to reflect the diversity of its community. Our workforce is dedicated to academic quality, student-centered programming, engaged citizenship, open exchanges of ideas, diversity and inclusiveness, and civility and integrity. We are looking for job applicants who share these values and commitment to our students. Worcester State is centrally located in Massachusetts, about an hour's drive from Boston, Springfield, and Providence, Rhode Island. Our 58-acre campus is nestled in the residential northwest side of Worcester--the second largest city in New England and home to 37,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Over 6,000 of those students attend Worcester State--approximately 5,300 are undergraduates and 1,000 are graduate students. Learn more about us at worcester.edu/about . Job Description: OFFICIAL TITLE: Staff Assistant, Design Artist SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Director of Publications & Printing Services SUPERVISION EXERCISED: N/A General Statement of Duties: The Staff Assistant, Design Artist serves as the lead graphic designer for Worcester State University and plays an important role in maintaining the integrity of the university's brand and serving as a creative resource for the university. The designer works within the Publications and Printing Department and collaborates closely with an inhouse offset printing team to ensure completion of design projects from concept through prepress. The design artist collaborates with departments and divisions to conceptualize and create various print and digital materials that tell the university story, including but not limited to brochures, flyers, postcards, digital images, graphics such as campus maps, posters, signs etc. for university departments, divisions and schools. Additionally, the design artist works with external vendors such as printers. Materials created by the design artist will educate, engage, and inform external and internal audiences, and support the university's brand. The position reports to the Director of Publications and Printing Services and works with the Communications and Marketing team as directed. Responsibilities: (E) = Essential (E) Creates original designs following best practices of typography, layout, photo editing and design using a Macintosh computer; all artwork created becomes the sole property of Worcester State University. (E) Provides expert creative guidance and design recommendations for campus projects. (E) Creates, layouts, and designs various types of publications, e.g., holiday and special invitations, publications, brochures, flyers, posters, large and small signs, business cards, signage, displays, postcards, newsletters, digital banner ads, social graphics and other jobs as directed. Additionally, creates artistic representations for campus e.g., buildings, special logos, campus maps and illustrations, etc. (E) Uses computer-to plate Postscript RIP station for outputting Polyester printing plates. (E) Has a thorough, extensive knowledge of Adobe's Creative Cloud programs including InDesign, Illustrator, and Photoshop; Google work environment; and Microsoft Office software. (E) Works with the Office of Communications and Marketing to ensure consistency in university brand and public image. (E) Exercises high standards of quality control for visuals, content and final deliverables (E) Manages and tracks all aspects of design projects, from time of design request to delivery of materials in accordance with the systems and processes established in P+P (E) Manages production with WSU Mail Center, and outside vendors as required; attends press checks as needed. (E) Manages and organizes all design project files on P&P Macintosh file server in a timely manner; updates department design job log(s) in Google Sheet. (E) Assists with graphics for the university as needed. (E) Provides expert creative guidance and design recommendations for campus projects; acts as liaison with external parties and vendor/contractors relative to design projects. (E) Ensures compliance with United States Copyright law. (E) Prints jobs to Xerox Digital press, Epson Large format printer and B&W production copiers; supplies a ruled-up printed setup sheet that travels with the jobs. (E) Assists Quick Copy with any special design needs for cover sheets for workbooks, course readers, etc. (E) Remains current on practices used by the graphic arts industry with regard to copy and offset preparation. (E) Demonstrates excellent customer-service, professionalism and collaborative skills. (E) Must be able to accurately step and repeat items on a page to maximize the number out and reduce the number of sheets used (E) Understands the Pantone color system and knows how to create designs for offset printing as well as digital printing. (E) Responsible for contributing to the WSU Strategic Plan. (E) Responsible for contributing to Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action objectives. (E) Performs other duties as assigned. Requirements: Required Qualifications: A minimum of 4 years' experience as a designer/typographer in a professional setting. Experience with copy and offset preparation with Macintosh computers. Mastery of Adobe Creative Suite, Google Applications, and Microsoft Office Suite. Experience with font management programs such as \"Monotype Connect\" Font manger for Mac. Demonstrated ability to translate concepts and ideas into compelling design materials across multiple formats and media. Excellent planning, organization, project management, and proofreading skills. Strong oral and written communication skills; ability to work effectively with internal and external constituents. Ability to effectively balance and complete multiple projects with competing deadlines, adhering to each deadline. Ability to successfully pass CORI and background check. Ability to perform essential responsibilities with or without reasonable accommodation. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in fine arts, graphic arts, or closely related field. Additional Information: This is a full-time, benefited, exempt, APA professional position. The firm annual salary range for this position is $65,000.00- $70,000.00, commensurate with relevant professional experience and education. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Worcester State University offers a competitive benefits package that includes: a choice of 8 different pre-taxed, health insurance plan options a choice of pension or 401(a), and supplemental plan retirement options life insurance up to 8x the annual salary long-term disability coverage dental insurance 20 vacation days accrued per year 15 sick days accrued per year 5 personal days per year paid holidays tuition remission for self, spouse, and dependent children and more... We are an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Members of traditionally underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Application Instructions: Applicants must apply online and may do so by visiting www.worcester.edu/careers, and submitting a cover letter, resume, and the contact information of three professional references. For assistance with uploading materials, log a ticket via the HelpDesk link, or contact us directly at 508-929-8053. URL: www.worcester.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203600": {
      "title": "Chief, University Police/ Director of Public Safety",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Chief, University Police/ Director of Public Safety Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Chief, University Police/ Director of Public Safety Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Chief, University Police/ Director of Public Safety Category: Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 740-Public Safety Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Aug 11, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 203600 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a liberal arts and sciences university with a long tradition of academic excellence dating back to 1874, as well as an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer that seeks to reflect the diversity of its community. Our workforce is dedicated to academic quality, student-centered programming, engaged citizenship, open exchanges of ideas, diversity and inclusiveness, and civility and integrity. We are looking for job applicants who share these values and commitment to our students. Worcester State is centrally located in Massachusetts, about an hour's drive from Boston, Springfield, and Providence, Rhode Island. Our 58-acre campus is nestled in the residential northwest side of Worcester--the second largest city in New England and home to 37,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Over 6,000 of those students attend Worcester State--approximately 5,300 are undergraduates and 1,000 are graduate students. Learn more about us at worcester.edu/about . Job Description: OFFICIAL TITLE: Chief, University Police/ Director of Public Safety SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Executive Director of Emergency Management and Institutional Safety SUPERVISION EXERCISED: Directs and leads all University Police Department personnel that consists of Deputy Chief(s), Captains/Lieutenants, Sergeants, Patrol Officers, Detectives, Dispatchers, Institutional Security Officers, and administrative staff, as well as contracted officers, student employees, and professional service vendors. General Statement of Duties: The Chief of University Police / Director of Public Safety is a visionary, community-oriented public safety leader responsible for ensuring a safe, welcoming, and inclusive campus environment at Worcester State University (WSU). This position is charged with directing all law enforcement, emergency response, crime prevention, and public safety operations, with a strong emphasis on equity-driven, trauma-informed, and de-escalation-focused policing strategies. The Chief is responsible for the strategic and operational leadership of the department, ensuring compliance with all applicable university policies and local, state, and federal laws, including standards set by the Massachusetts Peace Officer Standards and Training (POST) Commission, Clery Act, Title IX, and other relevant higher education regulations. The Chief commands a force of 15 sworn officers and support personnel, and represents the University in key community, civic, and professional safety initiatives. Responsibilities: (E) = Essential 1. (E) Lead a 21st-Century, Community-Oriented Police Department Foster a departmental culture grounded in service, transparency, equity, and accountability. Embed community policing and procedural justice principles into all policies and operations. Promotes an interactive, positive relationship between the University Police Department and other departments or offices of the University, students, parents, faculty and staff with the goal of building trust and strong working relationships between the department and the community. Build meaningful relationships with local residents and city officials. Participate in justice programs, student forums, and safety outreach events. Ability to adapt policies and procedures to police reform measures on the local, state, and federal level. 2. (E) Emergency Response and Strategic Safety Planning Develop and implement comprehensive campus safety plans in coordination with senior leadership and community stakeholders. Conduct regular threat and security assessments and ensure campus-wide readiness for emergencies. Serve on the University's Behavioral Intervention Team (BIT) & Emergency Response Team (ERT) and may serve as Incident Commander. Coordinate with local, regional, and state emergency agencies in crisis planning, preparedness, and incident response. 3. (E) Policy Development, Compliance, & Accreditation Develops and maintains the Worcester State University Police Department mission and the policies and procedures in support of that mission, which is aligned to the university mission. Create, review, and update departmental policies in line with POST Commission guidance, CALEA, and IACLEA accreditation best practices. Maintain strong internal controls to ensure compliance. Oversee accurate, timely reporting of campus crime statistics and safety information. Responsible for preparation of crime portions of Clery Report. Collaborate with Title IX Coordinator and Student Affairs to support victim-centered approaches to safety. Use crime analysis and data dashboards to inform patrol patterns, resource allocation, and performance metrics. Publicly share information to build trust and transparency. 4. (E) Personnel Training, Recruitment, and Labor Relations Promote continued education among staff, including POST compliance and mandated training (e.g., de-escalation, fair policing, mental health response, trauma-informed practices, etc.). Lead recruitment of a diverse and skilled workforce; ensure compliance with POST Commission standards and WSU's goals. Oversees ongoing staff leadership development as well as disciplinary action, evaluation, and promotion in accordance with union and college human resource policies. 5. (E) Parking, Transportation, and Access Management Oversee parking enforcement, permit issuance, and appeal processes. Implement fair and accessible traffic and pedestrian safety policies in partnership with the local community. Oversee access control and camera technology on campus, developing replacement cycles and technology oversight. 6. (E) Investigations Supervise all internal and criminal investigations, ensuring legal compliance, transparency, and trauma-informed practices. 7. (E) Budget Oversight Prepare and manage departmental budgets, grants, and resources to support modern public safety tools, staffing, and community engagement. 8. (E) Other Duties as Assigned Perform responsibilities as designated by the Executive Director of Emergency Management and Institutional Safety and serve as an institutional safety resource. On call responsibility required. 9.  (E) Demonstrates civility and professional, customer-service oriented behavior, worthy of emulation by other staff and students. 10. (E) Responsible for contributing to the WSU Strategic Plan. 11. (E) Responsible for contributing to Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action objectives. 12. Performs other duties as assigned. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Bachelor's degree in Criminal Justice, Public Administration, or related field. Minimum of 10 years of progressively responsible police experience, including 5 years in a supervisory or command level role. Demonstrated experience with community policing & threat assessment. Must have successfully completed an accredited Police Academy and must meet and maintain all mandatory certification requirements by the Municipal Police Training Committee (MPTC). Out-of-state candidates will be expected to promptly obtain a permanent exemption from the MPTC entry-level requirements for full-time police officers, including successfully completing the Massachusetts Municipal Police Officer Online Exemption Program and examination and to become POST-certified within six months of hire. Must pass physical/medical and psychological examinations as required by the university. Must possess or obtain a valid License to Carry a Firearm in Massachusetts. Ability to pass a comprehensive background check and drug screening. Must maintain First Responder/CPR or obtain within a specified period. Valid license to operate a motor vehicle Excellent interpersonal, communication, and problem-solving skills. Knowledge of budget management specific to a public safety agency. Preferred Qualifications: Master's degree strongly preferred. Graduation from a law enforcement command or leadership academy. Experience managing accreditation processes (e.g., CALEA, IACLEA, or Massachusetts Police Accreditation Commission). Experience serving multicultural communities including multilingual skills. Experience as police in a higher education setting. Experience with labor relations, unionized environments, and collective bargaining. Possess an understanding of mental health issues, as well as the value of having officers trained as mental health officers and being front-line contacts in this area. Experience working with the Clery Act, Title IX, FERPA, and other campus safety regulations. Additional Information: This is a full-time, benefited, exempt, non-unit professional position. The firm annual salary for this position is $150,000.00, commensurate with relevant professional experience and education. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Worcester State University offers a competitive benefits package that includes: a choice of 8 different pre-taxed, health insurance plan options a choice of pension or 401(a), and supplemental plan retirement options life insurance up to 8x the annual salary long-term disability coverage dental insurance 20 vacation days accrued per year 15 sick days accrued per year 5 personal days per year paid holidays tuition remission for self, spouse, and dependent children and more... We are an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Members of traditionally underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Application Instructions: Applicants must apply online and may do so by visiting www.worcester.edu/careers, and submitting a cover letter, resume, and the contact information of three professional references. For assistance with uploading materials, log a ticket via the HelpDesk link, or contact us directly at 508-929-8053. URL: www.worcester.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202907": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer Category: Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 400-Institutional Advancement Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Jul 22, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 202907 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a liberal arts and sciences university with a long tradition of academic excellence dating back to 1874, as well as an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer that seeks to reflect the diversity of its community. Our workforce is dedicated to academic quality, student-centered programming, engaged citizenship, open exchanges of ideas, diversity and inclusiveness, and civility and integrity. We are looking for job applicants who share these values and commitment to our students. Worcester State is centrally located in Massachusetts, about an hour's drive from Boston, Springfield, and Providence, Rhode Island. Our 58-acre campus is nestled in the residential northwest side of Worcester--the second largest city in New England and home to 37,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Over 6,000 of those students attend Worcester State--approximately 5,300 are undergraduates and 1,000 are graduate students. Learn more about us at worcester.edu/about . Job Description: OFFICIAL TITLE: Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer-Capital Campaign SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Vice President for University Advancement General Statement of Duties: The Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer (PGO) for the capital campaign serves as a senior frontline fundraiser responsible for driving the strategy, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship of Worcester State University's highest-capacity prospects. The role is sharply focused on moving elite prospects toward transformative commitments in support of the University's comprehensive capital campaign and strategic priorities. Reporting directly to the Vice President for University Advancement (VPUA), with dotted line supervisory responsibilities to the Director, Capital Campaign. The PGO operates with a dual primary mandate: Supporting the VPUA and the President on the institution's top, high-level institutional donor relationships. Directly managing a highly focused, elite portfolio of top-capacity prospects capable of making significant $1M+ principal gifts. The PGO helps build and sustain a robust, top-tier pipeline of leadership-level philanthropic support while driving sophisticated, donor-centered campaign success. This is a full-time, exempt, fully benefited professional position funded by the University's three-year Capital Campaign. Continued funding for the position is contingent upon the availability of campaign funding. Core Responsibilities and Essential Duties (E): 1. Principal Gift Strategy & Portfolio Management (60%) (E) Elite Portfolio Management: Manage a narrowly defined, highly focused portfolio of top-capacity prospects and donors (including high-net-worth alumni, friends, and key leadership figures) capable of making transformative major gifts. (E) High-Value Pipeline Acceleration: Drive the identification, qualification, cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship continuum specifically for individuals capable of six-, seven-, and eight-figure philanthropic commitments. (E) Customized Solicitation Plans: Build and execute sophisticated, tailored donor engagement plans, complex multi-year commitments, blended gifts, and high-level naming opportunities aligned with core institutional priorities. (E) Targeted Deliverables: Personally develop and maintain specific, critical deliverables, including: Comprehensive top-prospect strategies and milestone tracking Proposal concepts, bespoke case statements, and complex gift agreements Direct, strategic follow-up plans and clear \"next-step\" tracking immediately following donor meetings (E) Performance Metrics: Meet or exceed individualized annual fundraising, strategic proposal submission, face-to-face visit, and revenue goals established by Advancement leadership. 2. Institutional Leadership & Strategic Support (30%) (E) Presidential & VPUA Briefings: Partner directly with the President, VPUA, and senior leadership to co-strategize and manage the university's top institutional relationships. (E) Executive Preparation: Prepare high-level prospect briefings, strategic donor correspondence, and presentation materials for leadership ahead of pivotal engagements. (E) Academic & Leadership Alignment: Partner with the Executive Director of Development and Alumni Relations, Deans, and faculty to identify critical institutional funding priorities and convert them into compelling, high-level philanthropic investment concepts. (E) Strategic Coordination: Lead and debrief high-level campus visits, presidential engagements, and targeted cultivation activities for top-tier prospects. 3. Strategic Stewardship & Administrative Integrity (10%) (These are supporting functions focused strictly on the principal/major gift level; routine execution, event logistics, and broad reporting are handled by supporting staff). (E) Principal Stewardship Strategy: Design overarching, customized stewardship plans for principal and major gift donors to ensure exceptional, high-touch experiences and compliance with donor intent. (E) Data Integrity: Maintain precise, timely records of principal gift interactions, strategic next steps, and fundraising outcomes within the CRM system (Raiser's Edge). (E) Collaborative Liaison: Serve as a strategic liaison among leadership, select Foundation Board members, and key campaign volunteers to ensure alignment on top-tier donor strategies. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Minimum of seven (7) years of progressively responsible fundraising experience, with a proven track record in higher education, healthcare, or nonprofit advancement. Demonstrated success directly securing major and principal gifts, including six- and seven-figure commitments from individuals or organizations. Proven ability to develop and execute sophisticated cultivation, solicitation, and stewardship strategies for high-net-worth individuals. Exceptional strategic planning, analytical, written, and presentation skills, with specialized experience drafting donor proposals and gift agreements. Experience utilizing donor databases and constituent relationship management systems (preferably Raiser's Edge). Complete discretion, sound judgment, and absolute confidentiality when dealing with sensitive information. Willingness and ability to travel frequently and work occasional evenings and weekends to facilitate high-level donor engagements. Preferred Qualifications: Bachelor's degree or Master's degree or other advanced credentials. 10+ years of equivalent experience. Additional Information: This is a full-time, benefited, exempt, non-unit professional position. The firm annual salary range for this position is $130,000- $140,000, commensurate with relevant professional experience and education. Funding for this position is secured for the length of the University's three-year Capital Campaign. Continued funding for the position is contingent upon the availability of campaign funding. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Worcester State University offers a competitive benefits package that includes: a choice of 8 different pre-taxed, health insurance plan options a choice of pension or 401(a), and supplemental plan retirement options life insurance up to 8x the annual salary long-term disability coverage dental insurance 20 vacation days accrued per year 15 sick days accrued per year 5 personal days per year paid holidays tuition remission for self, spouse, and dependent children and more... We are an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Members of traditionally underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Application Instructions: Applicants must apply online and may do so by visiting www.worcester.edu/careers, and submitting a cover letter, resume, and the contact information of three professional references. For assistance with uploading materials, log a ticket via the HelpDesk link, or contact us directly at 508-929-8053. URL: www.worcester.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202905": {
      "title": "Director, Capital Campaign",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Director, Capital Campaign Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Director, Capital Campaign Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director, Capital Campaign Category: Administrators Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 400-Institutional Advancement Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Jul 22, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 202905 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a liberal arts and sciences university with a long tradition of academic excellence dating back to 1874, as well as an Affirmative Action/Equal Opportunity Employer that seeks to reflect the diversity of its community. Our workforce is dedicated to academic quality, student-centered programming, engaged citizenship, open exchanges of ideas, diversity and inclusiveness, and civility and integrity. We are looking for job applicants who share these values and commitment to our students. Worcester State is centrally located in Massachusetts, about an hour's drive from Boston, Springfield, and Providence, Rhode Island. Our 58-acre campus is nestled in the residential northwest side of Worcester--the second largest city in New England and home to 37,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Over 6,000 of those students attend Worcester State--approximately 5,300 are undergraduates and 1,000 are graduate students. Learn more about us at worcester.edu/about . Job Description: OFFICIAL TITLE: Director, Capital Campaign SUPERVISION RECEIVED: Vice President for University Advancement (VPUA) General Statement of Duties: Reporting to the Vice President for University Advancement (VPUA), the Director, Capital Campaign (DCC) coordinates the Capital Campaign planning, implementation, reporting, volunteer engagement, leadership briefings, and prospect management in support of institutional fundraising priorities. In this role, the DCC will work closely with the Executive Directors, Alumni Relations and Development, and the Senior Director of Advancement Services, to act as a strategic leader and steward for the University's comprehensive capital campaign and is responsible for campaign execution. In addition, the DCC will work with University leadership, advancement staff, campaign counsel, trustees, and Volunteers. Alongside the Executive Director, Principal Gift Officer (PGO), the DCC will specifically oversee campaign coordination - timelines, communications, and reporting systems. The position serves as the primary staff support to the VPUA on campaign matters and will be tangentially involved in all aspects of University Advancement fundraising activities. This position requires strong strategic, analytical, organizational, and interpersonal skills, as well as the ability to manage multiple priorities in a collaborative environment. Occasional evening and weekend work is required. This is a full-time, exempt, fully benefited professional position funded by the University's three-year Capital Campaign. Continued funding for the position is contingent upon the availability of campaign funding. Core Responsibilities and Essential Duties (E): 1. Campaign Strategy and Design (E) Collaborate with the VPUA, University leadership, campaign counsel, trustees, and volunteers to implement and monitor the comprehensive campaign plan. (E )Work with PGO to develop strategic campaign road map in collaboration with VPUA. (E) Design and execute campaign timelines, campaign milestones, campaign meetings, and campaign related activities. (E) Monitor campaign progress and provide regular analysis, updates, and recommendations. (E) Assist in developing campaign policies, naming opportunities, and campaign gift acceptance procedures. ?2. Prospect Management and Volunteer Engagement (E) Oversee systems related to campaign prospect identification, cultivation, solicitation, stewardship, and campaign pipeline management. (E) Partner with Advancement Services staff to maintain accurate campaign gift tables, forecasts, and campaign reporting. (E) Develop campaign strategies for principal and major gift prospects. (E) Coordinate campaign committees and volunteer activities, including recruitment, training, recognition, and meeting support. (E) Prepare campaign prospect briefings, reports, solicitation materials, and campaign communications for leadership and volunteers. 3. Communications and Reporting (E) Collaborate with Communications and Donor Relations staff to ensure consistent campaign messaging and updates. (E )Partner in developing campaign materials, including the case for support, campaign stewardship materials, donor communications, and presentations. (E) Oversee campaign dashboards, analytics, and reporting systems. (E) Create and maintain metric-based reporting tools to measure campaign progress. (E) Produce campaign development, financial, stewardship, and donor reports, including campaign donor and fund listings for annual reports. (E) Analyze campaign fundraising and benchmarking data to identify trends, opportunities, and operational improvements. (E) Maintain high standards for data integrity, financial tracking, and campaign documentation. 4. Staff Collaboration and Stewardship (E) Work collaboratively across University departments to support campaign priorities. (E) Participate in the development and implementation of divisional policies and procedures. (E) Represent University Advancement in campaign-related meetings, events, and donor engagement activities as assigned. Requirements: Required Qualifications: At least five (5) years of progressively responsible professional experience in fundraising, advancement operations, campaign coordination, project management, donor relations, or higher education advancement. Experience supporting capital or comprehensive campaigns preferred. Strong project management, analytical, organizational, and communication skills. Experience with CRM systems, fundraising analytics, dashboards, and reporting tools. Ability to manage multiple priorities and work effectively with senior leadership, trustees, volunteers, donors, and University stakeholders. Demonstrated professionalism, discretion, sound judgment, and commitment to confidentiality. Preferred Qualification: Bachelor's degree preferred; advanced degree desirable. Additional Information: This is a full-time, benefited, exempt, non-unit professional position. The firm annual salary range for this position is $80,000- $90,000, commensurate with relevant professional experience and education. Funding for this position is secured for the length of the University's three-year Capital Campaign. Continued funding for the position is contingent upon the availability of campaign funding. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Worcester State University offers a competitive benefits package that includes: a choice of 8 different pre-taxed, health insurance plan options a choice of pension or 401(a), and supplemental plan retirement options life insurance up to 8x the annual salary long-term disability coverage dental insurance 20 vacation days accrued per year 15 sick days accrued per year 5 personal days per year paid holidays tuition remission for self, spouse, and dependent children and more... We are an equal opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Members of traditionally underrepresented groups are strongly encouraged to apply. Application Instructions: Applicants must apply online and may do so by visiting www.worcester.edu/careers, and submitting a cover letter, resume, and the contact information of three professional references. For assistance with uploading materials, log a ticket via the HelpDesk link, or contact us directly at 508-929-8053. URL: www.worcester.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202947": {
      "title": "Visual & Performing Arts - Adjunct Pool",
      "description": "Worcester State University\nJob Posting: Visual & Performing  Arts - Adjunct Pool Worcester State University \n\tJob Posting: Visual & Performing  Arts - Adjunct Pool Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Visual & Performing  Arts - Adjunct Pool Category: Day and Evening Adjunct Pools Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: 673-Visual/Performing Arts Locations: Worcester, MA Posted: Jul 23, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: PT No Benefits Position ID: 202947 Share About Worcester State University: WORCESTER STATE UNIVERSITY is a vibrant public university located in the residential west side of Worcester, Massachusetts, the second largest city in New England and home to 38,000 college students at more than a dozen colleges and universities. Situated on a compact 58-acre campus, the University offers 50 undergraduate and graduate academic programs to more than 6,400 students annually. The Princeton Review ranks us as one of the 75 \"Best Value\" public colleges in the country, as well as a \"Best in the Northeast\" college. Of the 13 undergraduate public universities in the Commonwealth of Massachusetts, Worcester State University is one of only three to earn the latter distinction, which is largely based on academic quality and student surveys. Our tag line, \"Change the way the world works,\" speaks to our commitment to graduating students who are not only prepared for work or further study, but who also have learned how they can make a difference. With a marketing slogan of \"Academic excellence at an affordable price,\" the University strives to provide the best academic experience to students from all walks of life. Job Description: The Visual & Performing Arts Department seeks qualified individuals to join our adjunct hiring pool to express their interest in future, part-time teaching assignments at Worcester State. Typical courses specializes in Art, Music and Theatre, with the possibility of teaching courses listed in the current catalog. Total credits will vary from 1 - 9 in the day division, DGCE, or a combination thereof. Requirements: Art: minimum, MA; preferred, M.F.A. for studio / PhD for history Music: minimum M.M.; preferred, D.M.A. for performance /PhD for musicology, theory, technologies and composition Theatre: minimum ,MA; preferred ,M.F.A. for performance and design / PhD for history or dramaturgy. Dance: MA minimum; M.F.A preferred. Experience with distance learning preferred. Must have a laptop and reliable internet access. Additional Information: Worcester State University is an Equal Opportunity/Affirmative Action Employer. M/F/D/V. Women and minorities are strongly encouraged to apply. Here at Worcester State University, it is strongly recommended that all employees be vaccinated against COVID-19 before they begin employment. Application submission to the part time adjunct faculty pool is open to qualified professionals who possess skills, education, and experience in academic and/or professional areas. Adjunct faculty postings are for potential part-time, non-benefited, teaching positions during the 2023-2024 academic year. The applicant pool will be reviewed by the department chair, when searching for candidates to fill the position. Applicants for adjunct positions will not be notified of their status unless they are contacted by the department chair or their designee. Applicants should withdraw their application from the pool if they are no longer interested. Application Instructions: Please submit a CV, cover letter (noting interest in day, evening, or both), and one letter of reference dated within the last 6 months. Educational background will be verified through the National Student Clearinghouse or through copies of original, sealed transcripts if selected for hire. Documents which cannot be uploaded to one's account may be sent via fax to 508.929.8169 or by mail to the following address: Director of Human Resources Worcester State University 486 Chandler Street Worcester MA 01602-2597 Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation Worcester State University 486 Chandler St, Worcester, MA 01602"
    },
    "https://worcester.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=193869": {
